              DIGITAL AND OPENWARE TECHNOLOGIES, INC.
                         TO PROVIDE UNISYS
               MAINFRAME USERS ALPHA AXP ALTERNATIVE


MAYNARD, Mass. -- August 2, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
and Openware Technologies, Inc., of Jacksonville, Florida, today 
announced a joint marketing and development agreement to port 
Openware's popular XGEN 4GL software to Digital's Alpha AXP family 
of computers running the DEC OSF/1 UNIX based operating system.

     As a result of this agreement, UNISYS A and V Series mainframe 
users will be able to integrate their existing mainframe 
applications with new XGEN based applications running on Digital 
Alpha AXP systems. In addition, XGEN users on IBM, Hewlett-Packard, 
Sun Microsystems, and other UNIX platforms will be able to move 
easily and quickly to Alpha AXP systems.

     "By moving their applications to Alpha AXP systems, customers 
will enjoy mainframe-class performance and functionality at costs 
previously associated with minicomputer systems," said Jaime 
Ellertson, president of Openware Technologies. 

     Digital's Alpha AXP family of binary-compatible workstations 
and servers employs the world's most powerful microprocessor 
architecture to deliver superior performance and scalability.  
Alpha AXP systems are available in one- to six-processor 
configurations and offer processing power unmatched in each price 
class. 

     The XGEN toolset will run on the DEC OSF/1 operating system, 
the first general-purpose, 64-bit UNIX based operating system.  
The DEC OSF/1 platform offers a robust, complete commercial 
operating environment.  DEC OSF/1 software supports symmetric 
multiprocessing, a wide range of cluster functionality, and total 
compatibility across Digital's family of Alpha AXP workstations and 
servers, from desktop systems to large multiprocessing systems.  

     The DEC OSF/1 platform offers a complete System V environment, 
support for full 64-bit addressing to meet customer needs for large 
data structures, and the highest level of conformance to the SPEC 
1170 Specification, which will become the measure against which 
UNIX implementations will be tested by X/Open.

     "Digital offers the most standards-compliant UNIX 
implementation in the industry," said John O'Keefe, vice president 
of UNIX Systems Marketing. "Full conformance with the XPG4 brand is 
testimony to the progress we have made to assure our customers of 
the interoperability, portability, and quality they have come to 
expect from the UNIX operating system."

     "We are impressed with the performance of the Alpha AXP 
technology and feel confident that it will provide XGEN users an 
extremely attractive mainframe alternative in terms of price and 
performance," said Ellertson.

     According to Ellertson, the partnership will provide UNISYS A 
and V Series mainframe customers a powerful migration option for 
moving applications into a client/server, open systems environment.

     Openware Technologies, Inc., located in Jacksonville, Florida, 
is a leading supplier of application development tools, 
specializing in the integration of UNISYS A/V Series mainframes 
with UNIX platforms.  The company is committed to providing Open 
System solutions with industry-best products and the technological 
vision to enable customers to protect their assets while investing 
in their future.  Over 5,000 user licenses are supported in over 
32 countries worldwide within industries such as Banking/Financial 
Services, Transportation, Software Development, Manufacturing, 
Telecommunications, and Government.

     Digital Equipment Corporation, with headquarters in Maynard, 
Massachusetts, is the world's leader in open client/server 
solutions from personal computing to integrated, worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with 
industry-focused solutions from Digital's Business Partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
