                 DIGITAL RECEIVES R&D 100 AWARD FOR
                      GIGASWITCH/FDDI PRODUCT


     ...World's only FDDI networking switch selected as one of
      the year's most technologically significant products...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- August 9, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that it has been awarded the 1994 R&D 100 Award 
from R&D Magazine for its development of the GIGAswitch/FDDI, the 
world's only FDDI (Fiber Distributed Data Interface) computer 
networking switch.  Selected by more than 75 scientific experts in 
a variety of disciplines and the magazine's editors, the award 
recognizes the GIGAswitch/FDDI as one of the year's most 
technologically significant new products. This is the ninth time 
Digital has received the R&D 100 Award, the first being in 1964 for 
the PDP Multi Analyzer.

     According to Tim Studt, R&D Magazine's Executive Editor, "All 
the judges felt that the GIGAswitch was an innovative product with 
performance more than six times better than its nearest competitor. 
Its throughput, speed, capacity, and low cost were the major 
selection criteria noted by the judges. That it works over FDDI 
without the need to wait for ATM implementation was also a 
selection factor."

     FDDI is today's network technology of choice for building high 
performance data networks.  GIGAswitch/FDDI further extends the 
performance of FDDI-based networks; it uses Digital's patented 
crossbar switching technology to eliminate the 100 megabit per 
second (mbps) throughput ceiling of FDDI networks.  GIGAswitch/FDDI 
is used as a central switch at the backbone of high performance 
networks.  It also serves as a high performance system interconnect 
solution for high performance workstation computing farms.  Several 
of Digital's customers are using Digital's 64-bit RISC-based Alpha 
AXP systems interconnected by Digital's GIGAswitch/FDDI, to get 
supercomputer performance at a fraction of the price.

     Since 1963, R&D Magazine has annually honored inventors 
and scientists around the world by selecting the 100 most 
technologically significant new products and processes.  
Corporations, government labs, private research institutes, and 
universities throughout the world vie for this "Nobel Prize" of 
applied research.

     Past winners include numerous well-known products that now 
are part of everyday life, including: Polacolor film (1963), the 
electronic video recorder (1969), anti-lock brakes (1969), the 
automated teller machine (1973), halogen lamps (1974), the fax 
machine (1975), Kodak Photo CD (1991), and the digital compact 
cassette (1993).

     This is the 32nd year of R&D Magazine's international 
competition that recognizes innovators and organizations for 
outstanding applied science developments and technological 
advances.  Winning products will be honored in a three-week 
exhibit at Chicago's Museum of Science and Industry which opens 
on September 17.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
